    21	
    22	                                      MEMORANDUM OPINION
    23	
    24	       The John F. Kennedy Center for the Performing Arts is an iconic landmark in
    25	
    26	Washington, D.C. The Modernist building sits on a seventeen-acre campus overlooking the
    27	
    28	Potomac River, nestled between Rock Creek Parkway and a tangle of intersecting highways at
    29	
    30	the edge of Foggy Bottom. It was designed by American architect Edward Durell Stone and, due
    31	
    32	to its association with the slain president and notable architectural pedigree, determined eligible
    33	
    34	for inclusion on the National Register of Historic Places in 2012.
    35	
    36	       For years, however, the Kennedy Center has needed serious repairs. Last summer,
    37	
    38	Congress appropriated roughly $257 million to cover “necessary expenses for capital repair,
    39	
    40	restoration, maintenance backlog, and security structures of the building and site of” the
    41	
    42	Kennedy Center. See One Big Beautiful Bill Act (“OBBBA”), Pub. L. No. 119-21, § 60025, 139
    43	
    44	Stat. 72, 157 (2025). Plans for the work proceeded apace until February 1 of this year, when the
    45	
    46	Chairman of the Center’s Board of Trustees—who also happens to be President of the United
    47	
    48	States—announced on Truth Social that the Center would temporarily close for “Construction,
    49	
    50	Revitalization, and Complete Rebuilding.” Compl. ¶ 52.
    51	
    52	---
    53	
    54	<!-- page 2 -->
    55	Case 1:26-cv-00981-CRC           Document 45        Filed 05/29/26       Page 2 of 42
    56	
    57	
    58	
    59	
    60	       In light of the abrupt demolition of the East Wing of the White House only three months
    61	
    62	earlier, President Trump’s announcement sounded alarm bells for many, including a group of
    63	
    64	historic preservationists, architects, and historians who have filed suit to challenge the work
    65	
    66	planned for the Kennedy Center over the next few years. As their opening salvo, the Plaintiffs
    67	
    68	(hereinafter referred to collectively as “DC Preservation League” or “the League”) moved for a
    69	
    70	preliminary injunction to prevent the Defendants, including the Kennedy Center Board and
    71	
    72	various federal agencies, from carrying out the planned work “until they have completed all
    73	
    74	required [planning and environmental] reviews, secured all necessary approvals and permits, and
    75	
    76	been expressly authorized by Congress to proceed.” Mot. for Prelim. Inj. at 2.
    77	
    78	       At this early juncture, the Court concludes that the DC Preservation League has not
    79	
    80	established that a preliminary injunction is warranted because it has not shown a likelihood of
    81	
    82	success on its myriad Administrative Procedure Act (“APA”), ultra vires, and mandamus claims.
    83	
    84	Sworn testimony in this case by the Kennedy Center’s recently-installed Executive Director
    85	
    86	indicates that the Center will not in fact be demolished or rebuilt—at least as things stand now.
    87	
    88	Instead, it is slated to undergo a substantial renovation, which, if the Center were a federal
    89	
    90	agency or if the project were to affect the fundamental footprint of the campus, would trigger a
    91	
    92	slate of planning, historic preservation, and environmental review obligations. Yet the Court is
    93	
    94	bound by the D.C. Circuit’s ruling that the Smithsonian Institution, the Kennedy Center’s parent
    95	
    96	entity, does not qualify as an agency for relevant statutory purposes. See Dong v. Smithsonian
    97	
    98	Inst., 125 F.3d 877, 883 (D.C. Cir. 1997). And DC Preservation League has not established that
    99	
   100	the currently-contemplated version of the renovation project goes so clearly beyond the Kennedy
   101	
   102	Center’s statutory authority or so clearly neglects applicable statutory obligations to satisfy the
   103	
   104	
   105	
   106	
   107	                                                  2
   108	
   109	---
   110	
   111	<!-- page 3 -->
   112	Case 1:26-cv-00981-CRC            Document 45        Filed 05/29/26       Page 3 of 42
   113	
   114	
   115	
   116	
   117	stringent standards for ultra vires or mandamus relief. The League’s uphill battle is made
   118	
   119	steeper by the burden of persuasion it bears at the preliminary injunction stage.
   120	
   121	       The Court nevertheless remains circumspect about the possibility that the Kennedy
   122	
   123	Center renovation will encompass more than its Executive Director has promised, especially
   124	
   125	given the paucity of concrete details as to the project’s scope. If the work is, say, more
   126	
   127	transformative than present testimony suggests or requires permits that the Center has yet to
   128	
   129	acknowledge or secure, the Court’s legal analysis might look substantially different. In other
   130	
   131	words, this preliminary ruling may not be the “final word in this case.” Beatty v. Trump, No. 25-
   132	
   133	cv-4480 (CRC), 2026 WL 712814, at *2 (D.D.C. Mar. 14, 2026).
   134	
   135	       Given the lingering factual uncertainties, the Court will direct the parties to confer and
   136	
   137	file a joint status report within seven days of this ruling that proposes next steps in the case,
   138	
   139	including the possibility of periodic status reports to elucidate the scope and timing of the
   140	
   141	Kennedy Center renovation project.
   142	
   143	 I.    Background
   144	
   145	       A. Regulatory Background
   146	
   147	       Washington, D.C. has been a carefully planned city since it became the seat of the United
   148	
   149	States government in 1790. See, e.g., Nat’l Cap. Planning Comm’n, Planning History,
   150	
   151	https://www.ncpc.gov/about/history/ [https://perma.cc/FU8M-PBUM] (last visited May 21,
   152	
   153	2026). In the early twentieth century, Congress established several federal planning agencies to
   154	
   155	ensure that the District grew in a coordinated manner, under centralized oversight. Today,
   156	
   157	various intersecting statutory regimes continue to govern the city’s growth and development.
   158	
   159	       Originally enacted in 1912, 40 U.S.C. § 8106 is the perhaps the most straightforward of
   160	
   161	Congress’s planning directives: “A building or structure shall not be erected on any reservation,
   162	
   163	
   164	
   165	                                                   3
   166	
   167	---
   168	
   169	<!-- page 4 -->
   170	Case 1:26-cv-00981-CRC           Document 45        Filed 05/29/26      Page 4 of 42
   171	
   172	
   173	
   174	
   175	park, or public grounds of the Federal Government in the District of Columbia” without
   176	
   177	Congress’s “express” authorization.
   178	
   179	       Congress has also established the Commission of Fine Arts (“CFA”), which is
   180	
   181	responsible for advising on the location and design of “statues, fountains, and monuments” in the
   182	
   183	public spaces of D.C., see 40 U.S.C. § 9102(a), and the National Capital Planning Commission
   184	
   185	(“NCPC”), which is the “central federal planning agency for the Federal Government” in the
   186	
   187	capital and “preserve[s] [its] important historical and natural features” through consultation
   188	
   189	and/or approval of various federal projects, 40 U.S.C. §§ 8711(a), 8722.
   190	
   191	       The National Historic Preservation Act (“NHPA”) establishes another framework for
   192	
   193	promoting the “preservation of historic property” owned or controlled by federal agencies. See
   194	
   195	54 U.S.C. § 306101(a)(1). The Act requires agency heads to “take into account the effect of”
   196	
   197	any of their “undertakings” (i.e. federal or federally-assisted projects) on “any historic property.”
   198	
   199	54 U.S.C. § 306108. In what is known as the “Section 106” review process, see generally 36
   200	
   201	C.F.R. pt. 800, agency officials must consult with the Advisory Council on Historic Preservation
   202	
   203	(“ACHP”) well before a project begins in order to mitigate the potential adverse effects it may
   204	
   205	have on historically-significant properties.
   206	
   207	       Finally, federal construction projects in the capital may trigger the environmental review
   208	
   209	requirements of the National Environmental Policy Act (“NEPA”). See 42 U.S.C. § 4321 et seq.
   210	
   211	Given the value in integrating planning, historic preservation, and environmental review, projects
   212	
   213	submitted to the NCPC are also subjected to the NEPA process. See 1 C.F.R. §§ 601.1,
   214	
   215	601.2(d). A federal agency that must independently comply with NEPA takes the lead on
   216	
   217	environmental review for its own projects, whereas the NCPC serves as the “Lead Agency” for
   218	
   219	NEPA purposes when it reviews applications submitted by “non-federal agencies,” see 1 C.F.R.
   220	
   221	
   222	
   223	                                                  4
