   237	       B. Factual Background1
   238	
   239	               1. Upheaval at the Kennedy Center
   240	
   241	       2025 was a tumultuous year at the John F. Kennedy Center for the Performing Arts.
   242	
   243	Shortly after retaking office in January, President Trump replaced several general trustees on the
   244	
   245	Kennedy Center Board and appointed himself as a trustee, as well. The newly-reconstituted
   246	
   247	Board promptly elected President Trump as their Chair. Around the same time, the President
   248	
   249	sacked the Center’s former president and replaced her with Richard Grenell, a longtime political
   250	
   251	ally. See generally Compl. ¶¶ 33–35.
   252	
   253	       Over the summer, the Kennedy Center received an influx of federal dollars through the
   254	
   255	OBBBA. Congress appropriated roughly $257 million to cover “necessary expenses for capital
   256	
   257	repair, restoration, maintenance backlog, and security structures” of the Center’s “building and
   258	
   259	site.” Pub. L. No. 119-21, § 60025. An accompanying House Budget Committee report
   260	
   261	explained that the funding would “address deferred maintenance and upkeep of the Kennedy
   262	
   263	Center’s building and grounds” and “[m]ore specifically” support “water system upgrades, office
   264	
   265	space improvements, electric enhancements, elevator upgrades, rigging replacement . . ., seating
   266	
   267	replacement, lighting and backstage improvements, hydronic system (water heaters)
   268	
   269	modernization, and bathroom upgrades, among other items.” See H.R. Rep. No. 119-106, pt. 1,
   270	
   271	at 1175 (2025). President Trump publicly touted the appropriation, claiming that “in the coming
   272	
   273	months, we’ll fully renovate . . . the entire infrastructure of the building.” Compl. ¶ 38.
