   719	       C. Procedural Background
   720	
   721	       DC Preservation League filed suit in late March 2026, soon after the Kennedy Center
   722	
   723	Board voted to shutter the Center for at least two years pending renovations. It has brought
   724	
   725	APA, ultra vires, and mandamus claims against a suite of defendants, including “Agency
   726	
   727	Defendants” (NPS, the U.S. Department of the Interior, Interior Secretary Douglas J. Burgum,
   728	
   729	the U.S. Army Corps of Engineers (“USACE”), and the NCPC) and “Non-Agency Defendants”
   730	
   731	(the Kennedy Center Board of Trustees, the Smithsonian Institution, and President Trump).4
   732	
   733	       Shortly after filing its complaint, the League moved for a preliminary injunction,
   734	
   735	requesting that the Court prohibit any proposed demolition or major reconstruction until the
   736	
   737	Defendants obtained Congressional approval and satisfied a host of purported planning
   738	
   739	
   740	
   741	       4
   742	         The Court uses the “Agency”/“Non-Agency” shorthand in recognition of the fact that,
   743	according to the D.C. Circuit, the Smithsonian Institution is not an “authority of the government
   744	of the United States” under the Privacy Act or APA. Dong, 125 F.3d at 883. By extension, and
   745	for substantially the same reasons laid out in Dong, neither is the Kennedy Center (or its Chair).
