   767	
   768	  II.    Legal Standards
   769	
   770	         “A preliminary injunction is an extraordinary remedy never awarded as of right.” Winter
   771	
   772	v. Nat. Res. Def. Council, 555 U.S. 7, 24 (2008). To secure one, a plaintiff “must establish that
   773	
   774	he is likely to succeed on the merits, that he is likely to suffer irreparable harm in the absence of
   775	
   776	preliminary relief, that the balance of equities tips in his favor, and that an injunction is in the
   777	
   778	public interest.” Id. at 20. The movant bears the burden to show that the “four factors, taken
   779	
   780	together,” support an injunction. Abdullah v. Obama, 753 F.3d 193, 197 (D.C. Cir. 2014).
   781	
   782	         This case presents APA, ultra vires, and mandamus causes of action. Prevailing on the
   783	
   784	latter two claim types is no walk in the park. Ultra vires claims are reserved for scenarios where
   785	
   786	a government entity “has stepped so plainly beyond the bounds of its statutory authority, or acted
   787	
   788	so clearly in defiance of it, as to warrant the immediate intervention of an equity court.” Fed. Ex.
   789	
   790	Corp. v. U.S. Dep’t of Commerce, 39 F.4th 756, 764 (D.C. Cir. 2022) (cleaned up) (“FedEx”).
   791	
   792	“To prevail on an ultra vires claim, the plaintiff must establish that (1) review is not expressly
   793	
   794	precluded by statute, (2) there is no alternative procedure for review of the statutory claim[,] and
   795	
   796	(3) the challenged action is plainly in excess of the agency’s delegated powers and contrary to a
   797	
   798	specific prohibition in the statute that is clear and mandatory.” Glob. Health Council v. Trump,
   799	
   800	153 F.4th 1, 20 (D.C. Cir. 2025) (cleaned up). The third requirement means that “[o]nly error
   801	
   802	that is patently a misconstruction of the [relevant statute], that disregards a specific and
   803	
   804	
   805	
   806	                                                  14
