   812	
   813	
   814	
   815	
   816	unambiguous statutory directive, or that violates some specific command of a statute will support
   817	
   818	relief.” FedEx, 39 F.4th at 764 (cleaned up).
   819	
   820	       As ultra vires review “could become an easy end-run around the limitations” of judicial
   821	
   822	review statutes, the Supreme Court has “strictly limited” its scope. Nuclear Reg. Comm’n v.
   823	
   824	Texas, 605 U.S. 665, 681 (2025). The exception does not apply “simply because” government
   825	
   826	action “does not comport with law.” Id. (cleaned up). Rather, the challenged conduct must be
   827	
   828	plainly “unauthorized.” Id. at 680 (cleaned up). As such, an ultra vires claim is often viewed as
   829	
   830	a “Hail Mary pass,” Nyunt v. Chairman, Broad. Bd. of Governors, 589 F.3d 445, 449 (D.C. Cir.
   831	
   832	2009), though the hurdle is not entirely “insurmountable,” Nat’l Trust for Hist. Pres. v. Nat’l
   833	
   834	Park Serv., No. 25-cv-4316 (RJL), 2026 WL 877779, at *6 (D.D.C. Mar. 31, 2026).
   835	
   836	       Mandamus is similarly an “extraordinary remedy, reserved only for the most transparent
   837	
   838	violations of a clear duty to act.” In re Ctr. for Bio. Diversity, 53 F.4th 665, 670 (D.C. Cir. 2022)
   839	
   840	(citing In re Bluewater Network, 234 F.3d 1305, 1315 (D.C. Cir. 2000)). To be entitled to
   841	
   842	mandamus, the plaintiff must demonstrate “(1) a clear and indisputable right to relief, (2) that the
   843	
   844	government agency or official is violating a clear duty to act, and (3) that no adequate alternative
   845	
   846	remedy exists.” Am. Hosp. Ass’n v. Burwell, 812 F.3d 183, 189 (D.C. Cir. 2016). “These three
   847	
   848	threshold requirements are jurisdictional,” and even if satisfied, a court may still only grant
   849	
   850	mandamus relief when it finds “compelling equitable grounds” to do so. Id. (cleaned up).
